setup-stopped = installation stopped: { $script } exited with { $status }
setup-not-with-sudo = run the installer as yourself without sudo — it asks for the password when it needs it
setup-root-needed = administrator rights are needed, and neither sudo nor doas was found
setup-bad-manager = ELANTP_SERVICE_MANAGER can be systemd, openrc, runit, dinit, s6 or none
setup-no-cargo = cargo is needed — install Rust from https://rustup.rs
setup-no-artifact = after the build there is no { $path }
setup-service-failed = the elantpd service did not start ({ $manager }) — look at its log
setup-manager = service manager: { $manager }
setup-service-running = the elantpd service runs and will start at boot
setup-service-s6 = the s6 service is in { $path } — add it to your service directory
setup-service-runit = the runit service is in { $path } — enable it with a link in the directory runsvdir watches
setup-service-none = no service manager found — run { $program } as root yourself
setup-next = Next:
setup-next-desktop = speed, tapping, natural scrolling, disable while typing and workspace swipes are set in GNOME, KDE and other desktops, as for any touchpad
setup-next-settings = your own gestures: copy { $example } to { $file } and turn on the sections you want — the service picks the changes up within a second; without that file the service leaves the touchpad alone
setup-next-guide = every gesture and setting is described in { $guide }
setup-next-learn = a symbol of your own for drawing: { $elevate } elantp-dev learn <name>
remove-usage = usage: uninstall.sh [--purge]
remove-root-needed = administrator rights are needed, and neither sudo nor doas was found
remove-kept = the service is removed, the settings stay in { $dir } — --purge removes them too
remove-purged = the service and the settings are removed
